{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1189778-defining-carthian-law","id":1189778,"title":"Defining Carthian Law","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-02-26T21:47:08-08:00","id":1189778,"title":"Defining Carthian Law","content":"I recently started reading Secrets of the Convenants, and there are a couple of questions bugging me right now, as for the title. It is defined that Carthian Law gives supernatural weight to law, and i thought that was clear, many powers use the political landscape to create clear supernatural reaction in game.<br /> Then i got to the Carthian Merits section and i could not ignore the similarities between Sell Out and Right of Return, from the core rulebook, in dealing with Covenant Status dots. While none of them has clear supernatural effect in game, just dealing with Status dots from a meta perspective, i can't understand why Right of Return of them is a Carthian Law merit, while Sell Out is just a merit with a Covenant prerequisite.<br /> <br /> What defines what is Carthian Law and what is just a Carthian merit? In there really any difference between them from a mechanical perspective?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2213-tittopaolo210","name":"TittoPaolo210","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"144"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-27T01:46:09-08:00","id":1189806,"title":"","content":"Both are merits, so mechanically they are the same. The difference is fluff. And in this case the clue to the difference is in the title: 'rights' are codified my law, being a sell out isn't. Hence one is a Carthian Law merit, and the other is simply a merit with covenant prerequisites.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2081-howalt","name":"Howalt","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2081_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"303"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-27T08:27:30-08:00","id":1189873,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Howalt</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1189778-defining-carthian-law?p=1189806#post1189806\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Both are merits, so mechanically they are the same. The difference is fluff. And in this case the clue to the difference is in the title: 'rights' are codified my law, being a sell out isn't. Hence one is a Carthian Law merit, and the other is simply a merit with covenant prerequisites.</div> </div> </div> </div>Doesn't that kinda demeans the whole &quot;supernatural weight&quot; stitch of Carthian Law if it's just about fluffing up effect that are also justifiable by &quot;weird political shenanigans&quot;?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2213-tittopaolo210","name":"TittoPaolo210","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"144"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-27T22:27:16-08:00","id":1190150,"title":"","content":"Well, it wasn't meant to demean - but even if it was, it doesn't change the fact that that is the essential difference between Carthian Law and 'normal' merits with a covenant status requirement: fluff. It is exactly the same difference as between Invictus Oaths and merits that require Invictus status: it's just about fluffing up a effect that is also justifiable by &quot;thorough bureaucratic shenanigans&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2081-howalt","name":"Howalt","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2081_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"303"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-28T04:19:15-08:00","id":1190197,"title":"","content":"Sorry, but i have to disagree. Up until now Invictus Oaths and all other Carthian Laws have tangible supernatural effects: ignoring or breaking vinculums, obstruct Discipline effects, enforcing behaviour with unnatural damage or other effects, even gifting discipline dots... Right of Return is the only one that can also be ascribed to burocratic shenanigan and that doesn't have any tangible supernatural force backing it up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2213-tittopaolo210","name":"TittoPaolo210","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"144"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-28T05:34:07-08:00","id":1190217,"title":"","content":"My guess is that Right of Return was lumped in with Carthian Laws because it's supposed to be a Carthian only (which is somewhat amusing) Merit, unlike other Carthian Status Merits that can be taken by other characters, or just refluffed to fit other Covenants. So, a mistake, simply put.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-28T06:48:53-08:00","id":1190250,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TittoPaolo210</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1189778-defining-carthian-law?p=1190197#post1190197\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sorry, but i have to disagree. Up until now Invictus Oaths and all other Carthian Laws have tangible supernatural effects: ignoring or breaking vinculums, obstruct Discipline effects, enforcing behaviour with unnatural damage or other effects, even gifting discipline dots... Right of Return is the only one that can also be ascribed to burocratic shenanigan and that doesn't have any tangible supernatural force backing it up.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you are misunderstanding what I am saying.<br /> <br /> You asked if there is a mechanical difference or a fluff difference between Carthian Law, on the one hand, and merits with Carthian covenant status requirement, on the other. The simple answer is that the only difference is fluff. Both are merits, mechanically.<br /> <br /> Now, if the question becomes: how do I decide, based on fluff, whether a merit should be a Law or just a merit with covenant requirement, then I'd say: does it have anything to do with any of the three powers of government. Is it a merit that prescribes something (an ideal generally)? Is it a merit that forces others to uphold some prescriptive ideal? Is it a merit that judges if someone upholds some prescriptive ideal? If the answer to one or more of these question is yes, then it fits the mold for a Carthian Law. If the answer is no, then it's probably better of as a general merit - which you give a Carthian status prerequisite, if you want Carthians to have access to the merit only.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2081-howalt","name":"Howalt","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2081_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"303"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-28T07:08:58-08:00","id":1190257,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tessie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1189778-defining-carthian-law?p=1190217#post1190217\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My guess is that Right of Return was lumped in with Carthian Laws because it's supposed to be a Carthian only (which is somewhat amusing) Merit, unlike other Carthian Status Merits that can be taken by other characters, or just refluffed to fit other Covenants. So, a mistake, simply put.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think i will go with this interpretation, based mostly on personal preference.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Howalt</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1189778-defining-carthian-law?p=1190250#post1190250\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think you are misunderstanding what I am saying.<br /> <br /> You asked if there is a mechanical difference or a fluff difference between Carthian Law, on the one hand, and merits with Carthian covenant status requirement, on the other. The simple answer is that the only difference is fluff. Both are merits, mechanically.<br /> <br /> Now, if the question becomes: how do I decide, based on fluff, whether a merit should be a Law or just a merit with covenant requirement, then I'd say: does it have anything to do with any of the three powers of government. Is it a merit that prescribes something (an ideal generally)? Is it a merit that forces others to uphold some prescriptive ideal? Is it a merit that judges if someone upholds some prescriptive ideal? If the answer to one or more of these question is yes, then it fits the mold for a Carthian Law. If the answer is no, then it's probably better of as a general merit - which you give a Carthian status prerequisite, if you want Carthians to have access to the merit only.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, i definitely misunderstood what you said earlier, sorry. Thanks for the answers.<br /> <br /> EDIT: also, i realized i could have worded the first question much much better. I should have said &quot;design difference&quot; rather than &quot;mechanical difference&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2213,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2213-tittopaolo210","user":"TittoPaolo210","date":". 02-28-2018, 10:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2213-tittopaolo210","name":"TittoPaolo210","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"144"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}